      "text": "Let's denote $\\alpha = 28$ and $\\beta = 8$. Then, $z_{k}^{28} = z_{k}^{8}$ implies that $z_{k}$ is a 28th root of $z_{k}^{8}$. This means that $\\theta_{k} = \\frac{m}{28}\\cdot 360 \\mod 360$ for $\\ l $ odd.\n\nSince $z_{k}^{8} = z_{k}^{28}$ implies $\\theta_{k} = \\frac{m}{28}\\cdot 360 \\mod 360$ for $k$ even. \n\nWe can denote the set of angles as $A = \\displaystyle\\sum{((k - n)(28\\alpha \\alpha - \\beta...1)) \\mod 360}$ for $\\alpha,\\beta >1$ or $A = \\displaystyle\\sum ((k - n) (28\\alpha - 1)) \\mod 360$ for $\\sigma$. \n\nWe also denote a 22 N = $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k = 1} (k(n - 1)) \\mod \\alpha$ if $\\alpha$ is odd and N = $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k = 1} k(n - 1) \\mod \\alpha$ if $\\alpha$ is even\n\nFor M-degree planets, we count $2N\\ / 360 \\times m$, where $1\\leq m \\leq M$ and $mM \\mod$360. The remaining angle for both planets, $p$ in degrees, is $(360 - N) \\mod 360$. This is used to compose the angles.\n\nWhen we mentioned $N$, $M$ and $p$, there were assumptions made for N only. These problems mainly revolve around algs-only approach. The algs-only approach is typically taught after complex numbers, but it is not crucial in the first year of calculus. \n\nBy the way, for eg = n = 3, a = 28 = 8 = 7, b = 7 = 7, $p = 17$ and a = $17$ means had we passed through some obstacles.\n\nChoose the correct sign.\n\n$+3$ at the end of $7$\n\nFor example, at the end of 25, 7 went around the block and set count = (1,1) because “7 went 1 units right and 1 unit up so the offset is 1 (units where the $ 20 ^ + $ doesn’t change starting degrees) and going around the block.”\n\nIn the above case:\n\nThere were 3 lines, 28 and 8 bounds. When we talked about 3-8 (there's a question but this will give you the idea of algebratricks), $P \\Rightarrow (s x = 3 0) (s x y \\rightarrow y mod )$.\n\nOnce you make sure you understand the exposition, solve the above problem.",
